al for November 13.","t_rec":"Nome has nearly stopped setting record lows","p_rec":"If Nome's climate weren't changing, every year would have the same chance of holding a daily record. Since 2000, Nome has set <strong class=\"sig\">21 standing record lows</strong>, far fewer than the 82 that chance alone would give, and <strong>92 record highs</strong>, close to the 82 that chance alone would give. The 2010s hold the most record highs of any decade (46). Nome's all-time high is 86\u00b0F, on July 8, 1968.","t_chg":"Nome has warmed, days and nights alike","p_chg":"Across 113 complete years: Nome's average low has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.30\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.15 to +0.44), about +3.5\u00b0F since 1907. Nome's average high has risen <strong class='sig'>+0.28\u00b0F per decade</strong> (95% range +0.16 to +0.41), about +3.3\u00b0F since 1907.","t_mon":"Lows rose in 6 of 12 months. Highs rose in 7 of 12 months.","t_rain":"Rain and snow: no clear long-term change","p_rain":"Nome's yearly precipitation shows no clear long-term change (+0.06 inches per decade, a range that includes zero). The 1991\u20132020 normal is 17.2 inches a year. The wettest year was 1922 (29.49 in); the driest, 1962 (7.42 in).","show_snow":true,"sig_bars":{"lo":true,"hi":false},"t_year":"2026 so far: 1 daily record high, no daily record lows"}}